WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
The dot product.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:05.871
It is the raw score hiding inside attention and vector search.

3
00:00:05.871 --> 00:00:07.233
Let's see how it works.

4
00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:13.267
The dot product multiplies matching entries of two vectors and adds everything up.

5
00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:16.833
The answer is a single number, telling you how aligned they are.

6
00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:19.575
Positive means the same general way.

7
00:00:19.575 --> 00:00:22.967
Zero means orthogonal, and negative means opposed.

8
00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:25.467
Here is the recipe.

9
00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:28.000
Line up the two vectors entry by entry.

10
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:29.600
Multiply each pair.

11
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.233
Add the products together.

12
00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:33.600
And you are left with one alignment score.

13
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.333
Let's test it on a satellite flying at seven point five kilometers per second.

14
00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:43.967
Thrust along the orbit gives a big positive score, twenty two point five.

15
00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:47.303
Thrust at a right angle gives exactly zero.

16
00:00:47.303 --> 00:00:49.633
No relationship along those axes.

17
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:53.453
And a retrograde burn gives minus twenty two point five.

18
00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:54.333
Fully opposed.

19
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:59.667
One common confusion: dot product versus cosine similarity.

20
00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:03.086
The dot product mixes in how long the vectors are.

21
00:01:03.086 --> 00:01:04.833
Cosine only keeps the angle.

22
00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:09.633
Cosine is just the dot product after scaling both vectors to length one.

23
00:01:09.933 --> 00:01:14.300
So if your embeddings are already normalized, both rank things the same way.

24
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.667
In NumPy, it takes one operator.

25
00:01:17.967 --> 00:01:20.033
This is the satellite's velocity.

26
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:22.400
And three possible thrust directions.

27
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:27.833
The at sign gives the dot product: twenty two point five for the thrust along the orbit.

28
00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:30.500
Here is why this matters.

29
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:39.233
When Mission Control RAG retrieves its top fifty chunks, the vector database dots your query against millions of stored embeddings and sorts.

30
00:01:39.533 --> 00:01:45.815
And inside the language model, attention computes a query key dot product for every token pair.

31
00:01:45.815 --> 00:01:47.600
Same operation, two layers.

32
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:49.566
Let's recap.

33
00:01:49.566 --> 00:01:52.500
Multiply entries, then sum, to get one number.

34
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.867
Its sign tells you whether vectors agree, are unrelated, or oppose.

35
00:01:57.167 --> 00:02:00.100
Cosine is the same thing on normalized vectors.

36
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.178
And it powers attention and vector search.

37
00:02:03.178 --> 00:02:04.567
Next up: eigenvalues.
